From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 21:40:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2: hold fewer capture buffers for H.264 and HEVC streams

The decoder told the framework to keep 16 frames in flight for an H.264 or
HEVC stream, which the framework turns into 20 capture buffers. At 4K a
capture buffer is 11.87 MiB in the guest and again on the host, so a stream
held 237 MiB on each side. 16 is the deepest reordering a stream can have,
and suits a decoder that keeps the pictures it reorders in its capture
buffers. This device reorders on the host, and its capture buffers hold no
pictures that wait for their display order.

The framework stops queuing work at the pipeline delay plus the output delay
plus 4, and the host releases a picture once more than the stream's reorder
depth R of them wait, so a stream stalls only when R exceeds the delay by more
than 6. R is at most 16, so a delay of 10 stalls no stream, and it gives 14
capture buffers: 71 MiB less in the guest and 72 MiB less on the host at 4K.
---
 README.md                      | 10 ++++++++++
 components/DecodeInterface.cpp | 18 ++++++++++++++++--
 2 files changed, 26 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index a06c201418358dfed79db2ae77907e178352aecb..21f95188bce0eb64bf93323628891f78960ef509 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -479,6 +479,16 @@ are still in use against the number of V4L2 buffers, and the decoder lets a free
 V4L2 buffer stand for a block it has not met, since the picture buffer is passed
 again with every queue operation.
 
+The output delay of an H.264 or HEVC stream is 10 frames. The framework adds 4
+of its own and sizes the capture buffer queue from the sum, so a 4K stream holds
+14 buffers of 11.87 MiB in the guest and again on the host, where the stock
+decoder's 16 would hold 20. The device reorders pictures on the host and its
+capture buffers hold none that wait for their display order, so the delay only
+keeps the framework feeding the decoder until the host releases a picture: a
+stream does not stall while its reorder depth is at most the delay plus 6, and
+no H.264 or HEVC stream is deeper than 16. A device that keeps the pictures it
+reorders in its capture buffers needs a delay of 16.
+
 ### Input Blocks
 
 The device takes a coded buffer in a dma-buf at least as large as the buffer
diff --git a/components/DecodeInterface.cpp b/components/DecodeInterface.cpp
index 270505bc3ca0b8713a5fef10fe6be5d01d13b51f..4a07b15656321e921ed3529313c42429bafd9f96 100644
--- a/components/DecodeInterface.cpp
+++ b/components/DecodeInterface.cpp
@@ -70,6 +70,20 @@ size_t inputBufferSizeFor(uint32_t codedBufferSize) {
     if (codedBufferSize == 0) return kMaxInputBufferSize;
     return std::clamp<size_t>(codedBufferSize, kMinInputBufferSize, kMaxInputBufferSize);
 }
+
+// The output delay of H.264 and HEVC, in frames. The framework also sizes the capture buffer queue
+// from it: this many buffers plus 4 of its own for smooth playback. A 4K picture is 11.87 MiB, in
+// the guest and again on the host, so 20 buffers hold 237 MiB on each side and 14 hold 166 MiB.
+//
+// 16, the deepest reordering a stream can have, suits a decoder that keeps the pictures it reorders
+// in its capture buffers. This device reorders on the host, and its capture buffers hold no
+// pictures that wait for their display order, so the delay only has to keep the framework feeding
+// the decoder until the host releases a picture. The framework stops queuing work at the pipeline
+// delay (3, with no input delay) plus this delay plus 4, and the host releases a picture once more
+// than the stream's reorder depth R of them wait, so it needs R + 1 works queued: no stream stalls
+// while R <= this delay + 6. R is at most 16 (the HEVC limit, sps_max_num_reorder_pics, is 15; an
+// H.264 stream without a bitstream restriction can reach 16), which 10 covers.
+constexpr uint32_t kReorderingOutputDelay = 10;
 }  // namespace
 
 // static
@@ -606,9 +620,9 @@ uint32_t DecodeInterface::getOutputDelay(VideoCodec codec) {
         // queued before being able to output the associated decoded buffers. We need to tell the
         // codec2 framework that it should not stop queuing new work items until the maximum number
         // of frame reordering is reached, to avoid stalling the decoder.
-        return 16;
+        return kReorderingOutputDelay;
     case VideoCodec::HEVC:
-        return 16;
+        return kReorderingOutputDelay;
     case VideoCodec::VP8:
         // The decoder might held a few frames as a reference for decoding. Since Android T
         // the Codec2 is more prone to timeout the component if one is not producing frames. This
